    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All 42 tests pass \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**, including the 3 new `Cp29Tests` and every existing checkpoint.\n\n## Summary\n\nI added an optional, region-validated `postcode` field to owners.\n\n**Contract & model**\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added `postcode` to `OwnerFields` (type string, `pattern ^[0-9]{4}$`, not required), so the generated DTOs carry it in both request and response, and MapStruct maps it automatically in both directions.\n- `model/Owner.java` \u2014 added the `postcode` field with `@Column(name = \"postcode\")` plus getter/setter.\n\n**Persistence**\n- Added a `postcode` column to all four schema files (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `postgres`, `mysql`).\n- The hsqldb `data.sql` uses positional `INSERT`s, so I appended one `NULL` per seed-owner row to match the new column (the test profile is `hsqldb` with `ddl-auto=none`).\n\n**Validation** (new `ValidatePostcode` function)\n- Optional when absent \u2014 returns without touching the body.\n- When present: rejects with 400 (via the existing `OwnerFieldsInvalidException` \u2192 400 handler) if not exactly 4 digits, or if out of range for the city's region: NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099 (city\u2192region reuses `OwnerMapper.CITY_REGION`). A city with no known region accepts any 4-digit postcode.\n- Wired as a step after field validation in both `owners.POST.yml` and `{ownerId}.PUT.yml` (city is known by then), under the same `transaction` governance as neighboring steps. `ApplyOwner` now also copies `postcode` so PUT persists edits.\n\nThe request contract stays backward-compatible: an owner created without a postcode is still accepted, which the existing checkpoints (whose minimal owners send no postcode) confirm.",
